{"benchmark": "ifeval", "task_id": "1000", "sample_idx": 20, "model_name": "google/gemma-3n-e4b-it", "content": "Raymond III Count of Tripoli ruled the County of Tripoli from 1154 until his death in 1199. He was a significant figure in the Crusades a powerful and influential ruler known for his military skill political acumen and diplomatic efforts. His reign witnessed both territorial expansion and internal challenges solidifying Tripoli's position as a key Crusader state.\n\n*Early Life and Accession* Raymond was born around 1110 the son of Raymond II Count of Tripoli and Ermengarde of Hauteville a daughter of Bohemond II Duke of Antioch. He received extensive military training and participated in Crusades early in his life. Upon his father's death in 1154 Raymond inherited the County of Tripoli a strategically important territory on the Levantine coast. His early years were marked by consolidating his authority and dealing with internal rebellions and external threats. He faced challenges from various Muslim rulers and neighboring Crusader states needing to maintain a strong military presence.\n\nRaymond III’s reign was characterized by shrewd diplomacy and military campaigns. He actively sought alliances with other Crusader powers particularly the Kingdom of Jerusalem and the Holy Roman Empire. He engaged in numerous conflicts with the Ayyubid dynasty under Saladin defending Tripoli's independence and trade routes. His military victories helped maintain the county’s autonomy and economic prosperity. He fostered a relatively tolerant environment within Tripoli attracting merchants and artisans from diverse backgrounds. His administration focused on strengthening the county’s defenses improving infrastructure and promoting economic growth.\n\n*Diplomacy and Alliances* A key aspect of Raymond III’s rule was his diplomatic skill. He skillfully navigated the complex political landscape of the Levant forging alliances and maintaining a delicate balance of power. He engaged in negotiations with Saladin at various points seeking to avoid large-scale warfare and secure favorable terms. He also maintained relationships with European powers seeking military support and financial assistance for his campaigns. His diplomatic efforts were crucial in ensuring Tripoli’s survival and maintaining its position as a vital link between Europe and the East. He understood the importance of maintaining good relations with the local Muslim population especially merchants and traders.\n\n*Legacy and Death* Raymond III died in 1199 after a long and eventful reign. He was succeeded by his nephew Raymond IV. His legacy is that of a capable and resourceful ruler who successfully defended Tripoli from numerous threats and fostered a period of prosperity and stability. He is remembered as one of the most important Counts of Tripoli a defender of the Crusader states and a skilled diplomat. His reign significantly shaped the history of the County of Tripoli and left a lasting impact on the region's political and economic landscape. His efforts to maintain a degree of religious tolerance and foster economic growth are also noteworthy aspects of his legacy.\n", "logprobs": [{"token": "Raymond", "logprob": -0.0021344278939068317}, {"token": "▁III", "logprob": 0.0}, {"token": "▁Count", "logprob": -2.50339189733495e-06}, {"token": "▁of", "logprob": 0.0}, {"token": "▁Tripoli", "logprob": 0.0}, {"token": "▁ruled", "logprob": -0.2371193766593933}, {"token": "▁the", "logprob": -0.20648594200611115}, {"token": "▁County", "logprob": -0.054002758115530014}, {"token": "▁of", "logprob": -3.576278118089249e-07}, {"token": "▁Tripoli", "logprob": 0.0}, {"token": "▁from", "logprob": -0.00023135847004596144}, {"token": "▁", "logprob": -2.5987286790041253e-05}, {"token": "1", "logprob": 0.0}, {"token": "1", "logprob": -7.033323527139146e-06}, {"token": "5", "logprob": -4.3748852476710454e-05}, {"token": "4", "logprob": -0.0015049570938572288}, {"token": "▁until", "logprob": -